the only thing more frightening than my  own prom has to be Brian De Palma's 1976  horror film Carrie and part of the  reason I find it so terrifying is not  the blood and gore CC SpaceX chilling  performance or John Travolta's for that  matter but the way the tension is  written into the script so in this  episode I want to break down the prom  scene from Carrie and figure out how  screenwriter lawrence Cohen and director  Brian De Palma created one of the  tensest sequences in cinematic history  but before we get into the fun stuff we  have to know the difference between  tension and suspense because knowing  this difference will give us greater  insight into how De Palma and Cohen  constructed their tension think of  suspense is a state of mental  uncertainty will Hans Landa discover  Monsieur Lapidus Jewish neighbors  beneath the floorboard will Clarice  Starling realized the person she's  talking to is in fact Buffalo Bill  suspense usually involves an element of  danger where we're constantly asking  ourselves what will happen next  tension on the other hand is an element  of suspense which gauges how much we  care about the characters when the  characters we love are put in harm's way  such as a bucket of blood about to rain  down on your custom Maine dress on prom  night  we are concerned for them that's tension  whether it falls or not that suspense so  let's break down the prom sequence and  see how these two tools work in unison  taking place near the end of act 2 the  prom sequence can be broken into three  distinct segments each serving a  specific purpose over its 25-minute  runtime  segment one I've aptly named heaven and  it runs the longest of the three at  roughly 12 minutes  here we see Carrie normally a social  outcast fit in for the first time she  continues to fall deeper in love with  Tommy shares not only her first dance  but her first kiss Tommy who  begrudgingly asked Carrie out at the  behest of his girlfriend seems to  actually fall for her it's romantic cute  at times awkward but for Carrie it's the  happiest she's ever been and that's the  point of this segment  to set up a happy contrast to the  impending doom  of segment to purgatory unlike segment  one which is completely devoid of  suspense or tension segment two builds  in and it builds it unrelentingly until  it's bloody climax but in order to  understand how and why this tension  works we have to pause for a second and  take note of a few things that happen in  the first and second act one the mystery  behind carries telekinesis leading up to  prom carrie uses her telekinetic  abilities seven times five of the seven  occurring in the first 20 minutes then  sparingly as an uneasy reminder of her  power and this was a conscious decision  on De Palma's part as he notes in cinah  fantastique magazine in 1977 unlike the  novel I felt that telekinesis was  basically a device to trick and I wanted  to use it as an extension of her  emotions her feelings that were  completely translated into actions that  only erupted when she got terribly  excited terribly anxious or terribly sad  for De Palma and Cohen who was more  important that her power be an extension  of her humanity rather than a gimmick  additionally we never fully grasped the  extent of her power nor does Carrie in  fact she goes to library to research the  change that's happening to her body a  change that runs parallel with the  physical changes happening to her as  well the point here is that kiri has a  power but we don't know the extent of it  nor what Carrie would do if she was  truly pushed into a corner  second and most important leading up to  prom Coenen stills and the characters  empathetic qualities or qualities that  make us connect and relate to them these  qualities are given to four particular  characters so that when the suspense  kicks in so does the tension because we  will be connecting and caring about them  these four characters are Sue Snell miss  Collins Tommy and Carrie herself  sue is really the impetus for the film  she is the one who feeling guilty about  her treatment of Carrie asked her  boyfriend Tommy to take Carrie to the  prom  she additionally stands up to Carrie's  bully Chris and generally once Carrie to  fit in one could say the quality that  defines sue is compassion now there are  other qualities associated with these  characters but for the purposes of this  video I'm just going to go over the main  ones miss Collins is similar to sue she  sticks up for Carrie by reprimanding the  class Carrie why has feelings makes an  effort to connect with Carrie that's a  pretty girl and a skeptical of Sue and  Tommy's decision to take her to prom you  know I can make sure that you don't hurt  Carrie wide anymore  her quality is protection tommy is  interesting although he is initially  reluctant to ask out Carrie when he does  he makes every effort to be a gentleman  he makes her feel beautiful and even  encourages her to get out of her comfort  zone  his flirting even leads to a kiss which  in my opinion is genuine  although Tommy is a 1970s version of her  bro you can tell by his actions he has a  good heart  Tommy's quality would be chivalry and  finally we have Carrie many believe the  reason we connect with her is that we  feel sorry for her getting bullied  I disagree however that this is the main  reason as that is more an act of  sympathy rather than empathy just  because something bad happens to a  character doesn't mean we care about  them yes it's horrible that Carrie gets  picked on and yes some of us may relate  to getting bullied I'll be it likely not  to the extent she does her dreams of  wanting to fit in  live a normal teenage girl's life break  away from her domineering mother and  experienced teenage love I find far more  heartbreaking and ultimately connecting  than simply feeling sorry for her take  away those things and you get a less  rounded or flat character for a more  detailed distinction between sympathy  and empathy check out my other video on  empathy and horror films so now that we  care about the characters and armed with  the knowledge of carries mysterious  power we can quite literally set the  stage for segment 2 purgatory I call  this segment purgatory because it's the  gateway between the fairy tale prom  found in heaven and the hell that is  about to ensue in segment 3 purgatory  starts with bullies Billy and Chris  hiding under the stage getting ready for  the prank  this shows the seeds of suspension and  starts the process of tension this is  followed by a two minute and 13 second  long shot that gives us everything we  need to know to probably trigger this  tension we know the vote for prom king  and queen is being rigged when normal  slides the real ballots under the stage  and that a precarious bucket of pig's  blood hangs ominously over the stage  it's our minds that puts two and two  together will Carrie get dunked in blood  that's the suspense caring about whether  or not she gets dunked that's the  tension when Tommy and Carrie are  announced as prom king and queen that's  when things really start to amp up this  increasing tension is accentuated in  that we view it from five different  perspectives four of which are  characters we care about the editing  cuts between all of these vantage points  this was written into the script as  Cohen uses the scene headings to dictate  the pace of the tension su stage wings  peering out farther to get a good look  unseen at first by her there's a cord on  the edge of the frame Tommy and Carrie  dialling as they're led down the aisle  past a blur of smiling faces the  applause swelling the lights get  brighter the music gets louder beneath  the stage Chris listens to the footsteps  coming overhead grasping the cord Billie  is hooked up holding it very tightly sue  in the wings watching the action in the  corner of the frame the cord we glimpsed  earlier the same one which Chris  clutched beneath the stage jiggles and  sue notices it move she traces its path  with her eyes seeing that it goes up to  a bank of lights concealing a single  silver bucket every  this lead up from the slow motion music  and editing Cohen wrote into the script  and these elements combined with De  Palma's direction make for a truly tense  scene eventually releasing as soon as  Chris releases her hands from the rope  Carrie  now dripping in blood and ridiculed in  front of her entire school takes us into  the final segment of prom hell this is  our catharsis or the emotional tension  built in the previous segment releases  the characters we care about are either  incapacitated killed off quickly or  escaped in the nick of time from this  point on it is nothing but blood terror  and revenge it is here we see the full  contrast between segment 1 as well as  see the extent of Kerry's power and how  far she will ultimately take it  Kerri could have easily turned into an  emotionless revenge story but because  Cohen and De Palma took the time to  craft the characters the film still  stands as a horror classic the tension  is high the suspense is riveting and we  get a scene that goes down in cinema  history til next time keep on writing  [Music]  thanks everyone for watching make sure  to subscribe with notifications on so  you know as soon as one of my videos  drops and if you liked it please give it  a like  [Music]
 

